{"author":{"login":"Copilot"},"body":"The `stats.attack \u003c= 0` and `stats.hp \u003c= 0` warning branches are unreachable in practice. `CustomShipDesign::derived_stats` clamps attack, defense, and hp to a minimum of 1 whenever the hull template is valid (game_core/src/state/ships.rs:74-78), and this function is only called from the branch that already resolved a valid hull. The only way to get a 0 value is when the hull lookup fails entirely, but `design_validation_warnings` here is only invoked after a successful hull lookup. Consider either removing these dead checks or computing them from the unclamped sums (e.g., checking that components actually contribute attack) so the warning has real meaning.","id":3321041704,"line":514,"path":"crates/game_tui/src/screens/ship_designer.rs"}
{"author":{"login":"Copilot"},"body":"Test name claims it asserts on \"zero_offense\", but the body only asserts that some warning contains \"empty\". With an empty `components` vec and a valid hull, `derived_stats()` clamps attack to at least 1, so the \"No offensive capability\" warning will never fire and is not actually covered by this test. Either drop the \"zero_offense\" half of the name, or assert on the offense warning via a path that can actually produce attack == 0 (currently none exists for valid hulls).","id":3321041740,"line":708,"path":"crates/game_tui/src/screens/ship_designer.rs"}
{"author":{"login":"Copilot"},"body":"`clamp_to_100(stats.attack/defense/hp)` treats raw stat values as percentages, but hull/component stats are small integers (typically single- or low-double digits), so these meters will almost always render at a few percent fill regardless of how strong the design is. They don't communicate relative power and may mislead players comparing designs. Consider scaling against a maximum across known hulls/components, or showing numeric stats with bars only for things that are genuinely percentage-bounded (like fill ratios), rather than wrapping `meter_line` around an unbounded combat stat.","id":3321041755,"line":479,"path":"crates/game_tui/src/screens/ship_designer.rs"}
{"author":{"login":"Copilot"},"body":"`render_colony_table` now always passes `focused=true` to `panel_block`, so the table is always drawn with the focused border style regardless of which sub-panel the player is interacting with. On the Empire Overview there isn't a real focus model between the table and the new detail panel, but unconditionally claiming focus is inconsistent with how other refreshed screens (e.g. ship_designer panels, colony role/build picker) use `panel_block(title, focused)` to reflect actual focus state. Consider either using `quiet_panel_block` here for consistency with non-interactive panels, or wiring a real focus flag.","id":3321041780,"line":665,"path":"crates/game_tui/src/screens/empire_overview.rs"}
{"author":{"login":"Copilot"},"body":"The Food meter mixes two unrelated signals: the label shows the net balance (`{:+}/t`), which can be negative, while the bar shows production-vs-consumption ratio. A colony with `Food -20/t` (consumption 100, production 80) will render an 80%-full bar next to a negative number, which is confusing — bar trending high while the label says deficit. Consider either making the label match the ratio (e.g. \"Food 80/100\") or driving the bar from the same balance value (e.g. 0% when in deficit, 100% when in surplus) so the two pieces tell the same story.","id":3321041804,"line":379,"path":"crates/game_tui/src/screens/colony.rs"}

